---

<LayoutDefault
  title="Smart Lawn — grgaGrow garden app — Gordan Grgić"
  description="grgaGrow connects soil history, seven-day reports, weather and sun/shade with a private garden journal. Explore the cloud app and the ordered ESP32 / RS485 hardware."
>
  <div class="lawn-notes">
    <header class="lawn-heading">
      <a href="/projects/">← All project notes</a>
      <p class="folio-label">HARDWARE + SOFTWARE + GARDEN / FIELD NOTES 03</p>
      <h1>Smart Lawn</h1>
      <p class="project-state">
        In development <span>Cloud app online · Hardware integration next</span>
      </p>
      <p class="lead">A small lawn. A closer look beneath the surface.</p>
      <p>
        What started as an idea for watering my 49 m² lawn is becoming <strong>grgaGrow</strong> — a garden app that brings
        soil history, weather, light and everyday care into one place. The cloud app is online, with public views and a private
        owner workspace. Two wired probes and an ESP32 controller will connect it to the soil.
      </p>
      <div class="project-actions">
        <a
          class="demo-link live-link"
          href="/demos/my-garden/?public=grgagrow"
          target="_blank"
          rel="noopener noreferrer">View Live garden ↗</a
        >
        <a class="demo-link" href="/demos/my-garden/?mode=demo" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer"
          >Explore the demo ↗</a
        >
        <a href="/demos/my-garden/?mode=live">Owner sign-in →</a>
        <a href="#lawn-hardware">Explore the hardware ↓</a>
      </div>
      <p class="note">
        Live: public readings, regional weather and sun/shade · Demo: simulated readings. Live and Demo need no account.
        Owner sign-in opens your private workspace.
      </p>
    </header>

    <figure class="dashboard-figure">
      <a
        href="/demos/my-garden/?mode=demo"
        target="_blank"
        rel="noopener noreferrer"
        aria-label="Open the grgaGrow simulated demo in a new tab"
      >
        <img
          src="/images/projects/smart-lawn/grgagrow-demo-overview.jpg"
          alt="Current grgaGrow Demo showing two labelled simulated soil sensors and a seven-day moisture chart with example garden events"
          width="1265"
          height="712"
          fetchpriority="high"
          decoding="async"
        />
      </a>
      <figcaption>
        THE CURRENT INTERFACE / grgaGrow, 30 October 2026. Demo soil readings and garden events are simulated.
      </figcaption>
    </figure>

    <section aria-labelledby="lawn-software">
      <p class="folio-label">01 / SOFTWARE YOU CAN EXPLORE</p>
      <h2 id="lawn-software">One garden. Three ways to explore it.</h2>
      <p>
        <strong>Demo</strong> follows the published garden setup with simulated soil readings and example activities.
        <strong>Public Live</strong> shows the measurements and garden context the owner chooses to share. Both are read-only
        and open without an account. The <strong>private owner workspace</strong> adds settings, longer history, the care
        diary, alerts and sensor setup.
      </p>
      <div class="feature-grid">
        <article>
          <span class="small-label">HISTORY & WEEKLY REPORTS</span>
          <h3>A week beneath the surface.</h3>
          <p>
            Follow seven days of moisture history and a report of hourly averages, temperature and missing intervals.
            The private workspace adds up to 30 days of history, metric and sensor filters, and CSV export.
          </p>
        </article>
        <article>
          <span class="small-label">REGIONAL WEATHER</span>
          <h3>Weather above. Soil below.</h3>
          <p>
            Open-Meteo forecasts put air temperature, expected rainfall and precipitation chance beside the soil data.
            Forecasts use an approximate public location and remain clearly separate from measured soil conditions.
          </p>
        </article>
        <article>
          <span class="small-label">SUN PATH & BUILDING SHADE</span>
          <h3>Follow the light.</h3>
          <p>
            A compact sun path and daylight playback move together with the garden model. Explore sun direction,
            elevation and estimated building shade. Trees, clouds and surrounding obstacles are not fully represented.
          </p>
        </article>
        <article>
          <span class="small-label">PRIVATE / GARDEN & CARE</span>
          <h3>A diary beside the graph.</h3>
          <p>
            Save garden zones and sensor positions, then log watering, observed rain and completed mowing alongside
            moisture changes. Scheduled mowing stays separate until completion is recorded. Demo uses fictional events;
            the owner’s diary stays private.
          </p>
        </article>
        <article>
          <span class="small-label">PRIVATE / ALERTS & HISTORY</span>
          <h3>From an alert to the evidence.</h3>
          <p>
            An alert explains what happened, which probe was involved and when it was detected. Open sensor history
            takes the owner to the matching chart and highlights that time. The cloud alert log is available; phone push
            delivery still needs physical confirmation.
          </p>
        </article>
        <article>
          <span class="small-label">PRIVATE / SENSOR SETUP</span>
          <h3>Ready for the first probes.</h3>
          <p>
            A guide records installation checks and an estimated field-capacity reference for each sensor. System status
            separates controller contact from received readings. Saving a soil reference does not automatically validate
            calibration or watering thresholds.
          </p>
        </article>
      </div>
      <div class="software-gallery" aria-label="Current grgaGrow application screenshots">
        <figure>
          <img
            src="/images/projects/smart-lawn/grgagrow-weekly-report.jpg"
            alt="grgaGrow seven-day report with hourly moisture and temperature summaries, missing-interval counts and clearly labelled example activities"
            width="1265"
            height="712"
            loading="lazy"
            decoding="async"
            data-group="smart-lawn-software"
            data-title="Seven-day report — simulated soil readings and example activities."
            data-index="0"
            tabindex="0"
            role="button"
            aria-label="Enlarge the seven-day report screenshot"
          />
          <figcaption>SEVEN-DAY REPORT / Simulated readings and example activities. Click to enlarge.</figcaption>
        </figure>
        <figure>
          <img
            src="/images/projects/smart-lawn/grgagrow-sun-shade.jpg"
            alt="grgaGrow compact sun path and garden model showing calculated sun direction and provisional building shade"
            width="1265"
            height="712"
            loading="lazy"
            decoding="async"
            data-group="smart-lawn-software"
            data-title="Sun path and garden shade — a calculated model, not measured sunshine."
            data-index="1"
            tabindex="0"
            role="button"
            aria-label="Enlarge the sun and shade screenshot"
          />
          <figcaption>
            SUN & SHADE / Calculated sun position and a provisional shade model. Click to enlarge.
          </figcaption>
        </figure>
      </div>
      <p class="stack">React · TypeScript · Vite · Supabase / PostgreSQL · Open-Meteo · SunCalc</p>
    </section>

    <section aria-labelledby="lawn-reliability">
      <p class="folio-label">02 / THE ENGINEERING BEHIND THE SCREEN</p>
      <h2 id="lawn-reliability">What if a reading never arrives?</h2>
      <p>
        A reassuring number is only useful if it can be trusted. The app checks freshness and time quality, preserves
        gaps in the history, and withholds watering suggestions when readings are unreliable. A dry trend must remain
        valid for an uninterrupted 30-minute window before the current logic suggests watering.
      </p>
      <div class="engineering-note">
        <p class="small-label">DEVELOPMENT EXAMPLE / A LOST ACKNOWLEDGEMENT</p>
        <h3>Send again. Keep one reading.</h3>
        <p>
          A measurement can reach the database even when its acknowledgement never reaches the device. The local queue
          model and ingestion code are tested together: resend that measurement, keep a single database record, then
          remove only the readings that have been acknowledged.
        </p>
        <p class="note">
          This verifies the software model locally. ESP32 firmware and real flash power-loss testing still come next.
        </p>
      </div>
      <p>
        The cloud workspace uses Supabase authentication and owner-scoped database access. A separate public projection
        exposes only the selected garden data; exact coordinates, private diary entries, alerts and calibration notes
        stay outside it. The owner can disable publication. Demo values never become real sensor records, and Live shows
        waiting for data until physical readings arrive.
      </p>
      <p class="stack">Supabase / PostgreSQL · Access rules · Idempotent ingestion · Local automated checks</p>
      <p class="note">
        The app and cloud services are online. Physical ingestion, soil calibration, ESP32 firmware and real flash
        power-loss recovery still require hardware validation. Watering thresholds remain preliminary; automatic
        irrigation is not active.
      </p>
    </section>

    <section aria-labelledby="lawn-hardware">
      <p class="folio-label">03 / HARDWARE — THE ORDERED BUILD</p>
      <h2 id="lawn-hardware">Two probes. One path to the dashboard.</h2>
      <p>
        The first build now includes the two soil probes, ESP32 controller, garden cable, power supply and cabinet
        components. The aim is a wired installation that can be inspected and serviced, with readings sent over Wi-Fi to
        grgaGrow. The parts are ordered; assembly, calibration and testing in the lawn are still ahead.
      </p>
      <dl class="hardware-progress" aria-label="Hardware progress as of 30 October 2026">
        <div><dt>Procurement</dt><dd>Main components ordered</dd></div>
        <div><dt>Next milestone</dt><dd>First bench assembly</dd></div>
        <div><dt>Field status</dt><dd>Not yet installed</dd></div>
      </dl>
      <p class="note">
        Reference photos below illustrate the component types. Models and appearance may differ from the ordered parts.
      </p>
      <div class="hardware-gallery" aria-label="Reference photos of hardware component types">
        {
          hardwareGallery.map((item, index) => (
            <figure class="hardware-photo">
              <img
                src={`/images/projects/smart-lawn/hardware/${item.image}`}
                width={item.width}
                height={item.height}
                alt={item.alt}
                loading="lazy"
                decoding="async"
                data-index={index}
                data-group="smart-lawn-hardware"
                data-title={`${item.title} — ${item.detail}. Reference image of this component type.`}
                tabindex="0"
                role="button"
                aria-label={`Enlarge ${item.title}`}
              />
              <figcaption>
                <h3>{item.title}</h3>
                <p>{item.detail}</p>
                {item.source && (
                  <a href={item.source} target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">
                    Photo: {item.vendor} ↗
                  </a>
                )}
              </figcaption>
            </figure>
          ))
        }
      </div>
      <p class="note">
        Click a photo to enlarge. Each image links to its source. Assembly and specification checks are still ahead.
      </p>
      <h3 class="hardware-build-title">What each part brings to the build</h3>
      <div class="hardware-grid">
        {
          hardware.map((item) => (
            <article class="hardware-item">
              <div class="hardware-label">
                <span>{item.number} / HARDWARE</span>
                <span>{item.status}</span>
              </div>
              <h3>{item.title}</h3>
              <p>{item.description}</p>
            </article>
          ))
        }
      </div>
      <div class="engineering-note">
        <p class="small-label">BEFORE THE FIRST OUTDOOR READING</p>
        <h3>Build on the bench. Then move into the garden.</h3>
        <p>
          Start with one probe, confirm its power and protocol, then give the second probe its own Modbus address before
          testing them together. The final cable route, termination and sealed joints will be checked as one assembly.
        </p>
        <p>
          Remaining preparation includes gel-filled cable-joint boxes, single-wire ferrules and wire labels. The PG7
          glands must fit the 6.5 mm garden cable, and the delivered probes must explicitly support the intended buried
          installation. Automatic watering comes after reliable monitoring.
        </p>
      </div>
      <div
        class="architecture"
        aria-label="Planned data path: two soil probes connect via RS485 and Modbus RTU to an ESP32 controller, then via Wi-Fi and HTTPS to ingestion, PostgreSQL and the authenticated My Garden app."
      >
        <div class="probe-row">
          <span>Probe 01<br /><small>Moisture + temperature</small></span><span
            >Probe 02<br /><small>Moisture + temperature + pH*</small></span
          >
        </div>
        <p class="flow-label">↓ RS485 / Modbus RTU · BITNER LP0170 cable</p>
        <div class="architecture-node">ESP32-S3 controller</div>
        <p class="flow-label">↓ Wi-Fi / HTTPS</p>
        <div class="architecture-node">Ingestion → PostgreSQL</div>
        <p class="flow-label">↓ Authenticated access</p>
        <div class="architecture-node app-node">grgaGrow / My Garden</div>
        <p class="note architecture-note">Planned hardware connection · Real readings pending · *pH is experimental</p>
      </div>
      <p class="note">Hardware snapshot: 30 October 2026 · Ordered components, with delivery and bench checks ahead.</p>
    </section>

    <section aria-labelledby="lawn-site">
      <p class="folio-label">04 / THE REAL GARDEN</p>
      <h2 id="lawn-site">A practical question, close to home.</h2>
      <p>
        I still water the lawn manually. Sun, shade and soil conditions can make nearby areas behave differently. I want
        to compare how quickly they dry, how the soil temperature changes, and how long water takes to reach a probe.
      </p>
      <figure class="lawn-cover">
        <img
          src="/images/projects/smart-lawn/lawn.jpg"
          alt="My 50 square metre lawn with a robotic mower, the future test site for the two soil probes"
          width="1600"
          height="1200"
          loading="lazy"
          decoding="async"
          data-group="cover"
        />
        <figcaption>
          THE TEST SITE / My 50 m² lawn. Monitoring is not installed yet; placement and cable routes must also suit the
          robotic mower.
        </figcaption>
      </figure>
      <p>
        This is the part I enjoy: connecting the interface to something physical, making the measurements
        understandable, and checking the whole path from a sensor in the ground to a decision on the screen.
      </p>
    </section>

    <section aria-labelledby="lawn-next">
      <p class="folio-label">05 / CURRENT STATE & NEXT STEPS</p>
      <h2 id="lawn-next">Measure first. Automate later.</h2>
      <ol class="roadmap">
        <li>
          <strong>Online now</strong><p>
            Public Demo and Live views, a private cloud workspace, history, weekly reports, a garden diary, alerts,
            weather and sun/shade. Software behaviour is covered by local automated checks.
          </p>
        </li>
        <li>
          <strong>Hardware integration next</strong><p>
            Verify the delivered board and probe documentation, implement the firmware, then test communication,
            calibration and recovery on the bench.
          </p>
        </li>
        <li>
          <strong>Connect and observe</strong><p>
            Verify device-to-cloud ingestion, collect real readings and compare both locations through ordinary watering
            and weather changes. Use those observations to review soil references and thresholds.
          </p>
        </li>
        <li>
          <strong>Consider irrigation</strong><p>
            Only after reliable monitoring: test a valve, shut-off behaviour and watering limits. Weather and
            notifications are available in the app; automatic irrigation remains future work.
          </p>
        </li>
      </ol>
      <div class="closing-demo">
        <div>
          <h3>Take a look beneath the surface.</h3><p>View the public garden, or explore the simulated demo.</p>
        </div>
        <div class="project-actions">
          <a class="demo-link" href="/demos/my-garden/?public=grgagrow" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer"
            >View Live ↗</a
          ><a class="demo-link" href="/demos/my-garden/?mode=demo" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer"
            >Try demo ↗</a
          >
          <a href="/demos/my-garden/?mode=live">Owner sign-in →</a>
        </div>
      </div>
      <p class="note">
        Project snapshot: 30 October 2026 · Live measurements pending hardware connection · Demo uses simulated data
      </p>
    </section>
  </div>
</LayoutDefault>

